1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:03,358
A king leaned on his spear
while a singer sang. The

2
00:00:03,358 --> 00:00:05,756
spear went straight
through the singer's foot.

3
00:00:06,206 --> 00:00:08,807
The singer did not stop. The king did not

4
00:00:08,807 --> 00:00:11,027
notice. Nobody in the hall noticed.

5
00:00:11,477 --> 00:00:12,779
They were all listening.

6
00:00:13,229 --> 00:00:17,559
Ethiopia's Church remembers that
singer as Saint Yared. So who was he?

7
00:00:19,509 --> 00:00:22,343
Tradition says Yared
was born in Aksum. His

8
00:00:22,343 --> 00:00:24,715
father died when he was about seven.

9
00:00:25,165 --> 00:00:27,974
His uncle, a priest named
Gedewon, took him in

10
00:00:27,974 --> 00:00:30,050
and tried to teach him the psalms.

11
00:00:30,500 --> 00:00:33,178
The boy could not keep
the lessons in his head.

12
00:00:33,178 --> 00:00:35,343
The other pupils moved on without him.

13
00:00:35,793 --> 00:00:39,569
One day he simply ran away. And
then he sat down under a tree.

14
00:00:41,519 --> 00:00:43,960
On the trunk, a caterpillar was trying to

15
00:00:43,960 --> 00:00:45,807
climb. Some tellers say an ant.

16
00:00:46,257 --> 00:00:49,435
It slipped and fell. It
climbed again, and fell again.

17
00:00:49,885 --> 00:00:53,448
Again and again, until at last
it reached the fruit at the top.

18
00:00:53,898 --> 00:00:57,546
Yared got up, walked back to his
uncle, and asked to start over.

19
00:00:57,996 --> 00:01:00,922
The tradition says he went
on to learn the scriptures

20
00:01:00,922 --> 00:01:03,351
by heart. But where did
the music come from?

21
00:01:05,301 --> 00:01:08,799
The Church's account says God
sent him three birds from Eden.

22
00:01:09,249 --> 00:01:13,367
One teacher's version names them,
and says they spoke to him in Ge'ez.

23
00:01:13,817 --> 00:01:17,443
They carried him up, the story goes,
and he heard the song of heaven.

24
00:01:17,893 --> 00:01:22,117
He came down in Aksum, at the church
of Zion, and began to sing it.

25
00:01:22,567 --> 00:01:25,939
Out of that song came three
modes: Ge'ez, Ezel and

26
00:01:25,939 --> 00:01:28,839
Araray. They are still
sung today. But how?

27
00:01:30,789 --> 00:01:32,909
Did you know? In church, this chant is

28
00:01:32,909 --> 00:01:34,693
sung from memory, without books.

29
00:01:35,143 --> 00:01:38,770
Scholars met one chanter who had
trained for thirty‑one years.

30
00:01:39,220 --> 00:01:41,751
The small notation signs written above the

31
00:01:41,751 --> 00:01:43,679
words are letters, a memory aid.

32
00:01:44,129 --> 00:01:47,749
And copies of the chant
book from before the 1500s

33
00:01:47,749 --> 00:01:50,934
carry no signs at all.
So who wrote it down?

34
00:01:52,884 --> 00:01:55,643
Back to the hall of King Gebre Meskel. The

35
00:01:55,643 --> 00:01:57,876
song ends. Someone sees the blood.

36
00:01:58,326 --> 00:02:00,917
The king pulls out his
spear and, horrified,

37
00:02:00,917 --> 00:02:02,742
offers Yared anything he wants.

38
00:02:03,192 --> 00:02:05,432
Yared makes him swear he will not refuse.

39
00:02:05,882 --> 00:02:09,167
Then he asks for the one thing
a king does not want to give.

40
00:02:09,617 --> 00:02:12,049
Send me away, that I may become a monk.

41
00:02:13,999 --> 00:02:17,697
Did you know? The oldest
manuscript we have with

42
00:02:17,697 --> 00:02:20,933
Yared's life is a synaxary from the 1500s.

43
00:02:21,383 --> 00:02:25,223
That is about a thousand years
after the date tradition gives him.

44
00:02:25,673 --> 00:02:29,392
One Ethiopian scholar, Getatchew
Haile, has even argued

45
00:02:29,392 --> 00:02:32,435
he lived in the ninth
century, not the sixth.

46
00:02:32,885 --> 00:02:34,912
So what is left that we can stand on?

47
00:02:36,862 --> 00:02:39,742
Tradition says Yared
went into the wilderness

48
00:02:39,742 --> 00:02:41,982
to pray, and departed on Ginbot 11.

49
00:02:42,432 --> 00:02:45,703
Some say he died. Some
say he was taken, like

50
00:02:45,703 --> 00:02:48,683
Enoch and Elijah. No one knows his grave.

51
00:02:49,133 --> 00:02:52,080
In the Semien Mountains,
two peaks stand on one

52
00:02:52,080 --> 00:02:54,274
ridge: Abba Yared and Qiddus Yared.

53
00:02:54,724 --> 00:02:58,059
Ethiopian Jews in Semien
also remember him as a

54
00:02:58,059 --> 00:03:01,039
composer of zema, and as one of their own.

55
00:03:01,489 --> 00:03:04,531
One elder told researchers:
we say he was Jewish,

56
00:03:04,531 --> 00:03:07,014
and the Christians say he was Christian.

57
00:03:07,464 --> 00:03:10,408
Two communities, one
song, one ridge of stone.

58
00:03:12,358 --> 00:03:15,938
We traced Yared through a
church reading, a 1500s

59
00:03:15,938 --> 00:03:20,102
manuscript, chant scholars and
an elder in the mountains.

60
00:03:20,552 --> 00:03:23,423
The birds are tradition.
The thousand‑year gap

61
00:03:23,423 --> 00:03:25,608
is history. Both are worth knowing.

62
00:03:26,058 --> 00:03:29,557
When a story sounds too neat,
ask: who wrote it down, and when?

63
00:03:31,507 --> 00:03:34,792
A slow boy watched a caterpillar
fall and climb again.

64
00:03:35,242 --> 00:03:37,726
We are tempted to quit
when learning is slow,

65
00:03:37,726 --> 00:03:39,658
or to chase applause when it comes.

66
00:03:40,108 --> 00:03:42,782
Yared did neither. He
kept climbing, and when

67
00:03:42,782 --> 00:03:45,100
the reward came, he asked only to pray.

68
00:03:45,550 --> 00:03:49,155
Maybe today we read one psalm
slowly, and let it carry us.

69
00:03:51,105 --> 00:03:53,196
Thank you for sitting with us over coffee.

70
00:03:53,646 --> 00:03:55,118
This is Ethiopics.
